Wait — I used `data-correct="true"` in the HTML quiz buttons. In the JS I wrote `b.getAttribute('data-c')==='true'` — it's a mismatch! It has to be `data-correct`. Let me fix it: `b.getAttribute('data-correct')==='true'`.

Also `nodeList.forEach` — modern browsers support forEach on NodeList. No problem.

`e.target.closest('.chip')` — could target be a text node? In DOM events, e.target is an element (or a text node inside a button? in browsers, the target of a click event on text inside a button is the button... actually, the target can be a text node? No — for mouse events, target is the deepest element, and a text node inside a <button> becomes the button? Actually, when you click on text inside a button, event.target is the <button> element itself (text nodes aren't event targets). Hmm, actually event.target is an element; when clicking text inside a button, target = button? In some browsers target could be the text node... no, text nodes can't be event targets; the target is the button or the .dot span. .dot is a span inside the button, so closest('.chip') will work. OK.

The chip's innerHTML uses buttons with the class .chip — those also match document.querySelectorAll('.chip') for the active toggle — I've already included that.

Note: showLayer re-renders the panel including the chips, and after innerHTML I toggle .active on .chip elements — no problem.

Another consideration: `panel.innerHTML=...+chipsHtml(id)` — the initial call showLayer('crust') sets the default panel to the crust info. Good.

The marker position: .depth-mark has right:0 by default; updateDepth sets style.right to a percentage. The strip is RTL: the right edge = 0 km. Good.

toLocaleString('en-US') gives "6,371" — with the Arabic comma? 'en-US' uses commas. No problem. Actually, it might be simpler without formatting: just d+' كم'. For 6371 → "6371 كم". Either is fine; I'll use toLocaleString('en-US') for numbers like 2,891. Hmm, Arabic texts often use the Western format without thousands separators. To keep it clean, I'll skip the toLocaleString: just use d + ' كم' and temp + '°م'.

Next I'll replace PART3 with this JS. Also I'll fix the "Mوجات" typo.

